\subsubsection{Degradation cases}
\label{sec:ou-robustness}
Two ten-seed degradation cases extend the main OU--III validation envelope
without changing the deployed SpectralMSE regularizer law.  The first uses a
low-motion $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$ record to expose the approximately fixed absolute
sensor-error floor.  The second compresses the controlled transition between
the same endpoints from \SI{120}{s} to \SI{30}{s}; both Adaptive and
FixedNominal are scored so transition degradation remains separated from the
fixed operating-point baseline.

\IfFileExists{w3d-ou-robustness-results-generated.tex-part}{%
  \input{w3d-ou-robustness-results-generated.tex-part}%
}{}

\IfFileExists{ou_robustness_stress.svg}{%
  \begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
    \includesvg[width=\columnwidth,inkscapelatex=false]{ou_robustness_stress}
    \caption{Low-motion and rapid-transition degradation cases.}
    \label{fig:ou_robustness_stress}
  \end{figure}
}{}

At $H_s=\SI{0.05}{m}$, the mean absolute vertical RMS error is
\SI{\OURobustnessLowStressAbsolute}{m}, corresponding to
\OURobustnessLowStressMean\% of $H_s$; the increase in normalized error exposes
the approximately fixed absolute sensor-error floor.  Compressing the
transition from \SI{120}{s} to \SI{30}{s} changes the Adaptive mean from
\OURobustnessControlledAdaptiveMean\% to
\OURobustnessRapidAdaptiveMean\% of the final $H_s$.  This rapid-case difference
is a degradation result rather than a pure adaptation-rate measurement because
compressing the crossfade also changes the composition of the fixed
\SI{900}{s} scoring window.  Isolating adaptation bandwidth would require a
protocol that holds the scored sea-state composition fixed while varying only
the transition rate.
